 \ReviewerComment{4. Please include uncertainty estimates for SHAP importance and feature-ablation results.}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{Uncertainty estimates are provided separately for training variability and test-case sampling uncertainty in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:interpretation_uncertainty}. We use the existing model-matched analyses of BiLSTM-Attention and BU-LSTM-Attention, each with the five fixed seeds 42, 123, 7, 2024 and 99. No new experiment, figure or supplementary table is needed for this response.
+
+For grouped SHAP, Figure~\ref{manuscript-fig:r1m4_shap_comparison} and Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_shap_magnitude}--\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_rank_stability} distinguish five-seed means and SDs from pointwise 95\% calendar-day cluster-bootstrap intervals (2,000 replicates, resampling seed 42). The latter resample explained cases after averaging absolute attributions across the fixed fitted models, while retaining the same training background. For example, BiLSTM-Attention upper-tail mntpr mean absolute SHAP is 0.089130~mm\,h$^{-1}$, with seed SD 0.005174~mm\,h$^{-1}$ and a day-bootstrap interval of $[0.075460,0.102467]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (Table~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_shap_magnitude}). These are attribution magnitudes: a positive magnitude or an interval above zero does not demonstrate independent predictive benefit or statistical significance of forecast improvement.
+
+For ablation, Figure~\ref{manuscript-fig:r1m4_ablation_comparison} and Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_ablation_bilstm_attn}--\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_seed_pairs_bulstm_attn} give the five seed-paired differences $\Delta\mathrm{RMSE}=\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{removed}}-\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{full}}$, their mean and sample SD, pointwise 95\% Student-$t$ intervals ($df=4$), and pointwise 95\% paired moving-block bootstrap intervals (28 six-hourly samples per block, 10,000 replicates, resampling seed 42). The bootstrap uses the same blocks for full/reduced models and across seeds; each within-seed RMSE difference is calculated before averaging the differences. It is not computed from an ensemble-mean prediction. Removing sp from BiLSTM-Attention increases overall RMSE by 0.004022~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (seed SD 0.000606), with a seed-$t$ interval $[0.003270,0.004774]$ and block-bootstrap interval $[0.002527,0.005547]$~mm\,h$^{-1}$. All five seeds deteriorate (Table~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_ablation_bilstm_attn}).
+
+The negative findings are also retained. Removing mntpr from BU-LSTM-Attention gives paired mean RMSE differences on the full, revised-wet ($y\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$) and upper-tail subsets of $-0.001901$, $-0.010509$ and $-0.020662$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, respectively (Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_ablation_bulstm_attn} and~\ref{supp-stab:r1m4_seed_pairs_bulstm_attn}). Each subset meets all three descriptive robustness criteria used here: improvement in all five seeds, a seed-$t$ interval excluding zero, and a paired block-bootstrap interval excluding zero. This is specific to the tested architecture, configurations and evaluation subsets; it does not show that mntpr is generally harmful or establish meteorological causality. In contrast, BU-LSTM-Attention sp and tcc removal increases full-test error in only four of five seeds: their block-bootstrap intervals are above zero but their seed-$t$ intervals include zero. They are not described as uniformly robust important predictors.
+
+The limitations are explicit. SHAP bootstrap intervals condition on the fitted models and do not include training randomness; sensitivity to a different fixed training background was not evaluated. The temporal SHAP analysis is a separate finite-permutation approximation without its own confidence intervals, and is not substituted for grouped attribution uncertainty. Ablation seed intervals are based on only five fitted models. Training and case-sampling uncertainty have not been combined into a total interval. All intervals are pointwise and unadjusted for multiple comparisons; the three-way rule is not a multiplicity correction. The existing manuscript and figures are retained unchanged. Final page and line numbers remain pending full-manuscript assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Existing training and sampling uncertainty (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:interpretation_uncertainty}; final page and line numbers pending)}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Training variability was summarized by five-seed standard deviations, pointwise 95\% Student-$t$ intervals with four degrees of freedom and counts of seeds with a positive removal effect. These seed-based summaries describe variability across five fitted models, whereas the bootstrap intervals describe test-sample uncertainty conditional on those models; neither combines all sources of uncertainty. All reported confidence intervals are pointwise 95\%, without family-wise or false-discovery-rate adjustment. For ablation, test-sample uncertainty was estimated using paired moving blocks of 28 consecutive six-hourly samples (seven days), with 10,000 replicates and resampling seed 42 specified in the archived analysis code. The same sampled blocks were used for the full and reduced configurations and across seeds; RMSE differences were calculated within each seed and then averaged, rather than first averaging predictions across seeds. For SHAP, sampling intervals used 2,000 bootstrap replicates resampling explained cases by calendar day, with resampling seed 42 specified in the code; the fixed background was not resampled. These intervals address test-case sampling uncertainty and do not evaluate sensitivity to alternative backgrounds.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Existing architecture-dependent interval disagreement and negative effects (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:ablation_results}; final page and line numbers pending)}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The BU-LSTM-Attention results were less stable across training seeds. Removing tcc or sp increased RMSE in four of five seeds; their pointwise 95\% moving-block bootstrap intervals excluded zero, but their seed-$t$ intervals included zero. They are therefore not described as equally robust to the BiLSTM-Attention effects. Removing individual precipitation-related predictors sometimes improved performance; for mntpr, the overall mean difference was negative and both pointwise intervals excluded zero. Overall ablation-rank agreement across seeds was 0.867 for BiLSTM-Attention and 0.543 for BU-LSTM-Attention, further indicating architecture-dependent training variability.}
+}
 
